{"name":"Hagstone Choir","hex":"#8b837d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hagstone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An adder stone, a stone with a naturally occurring hole through it.","A ceremonial stone with a hole drilled into used to ward off evil or see into other worlds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hagstone"},{"term":"choir","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A group of people who sing together; a company of people who are trained to sing together.","One quarter of a cruciform church, or the architectural area of a church, generally used by the choir; often near the apse."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sing in concert."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/choir"}],"entries":[{"title":"Adder stone","blocks":[{"text":"An adder stone is a type of stone, usually glassy, with a naturally occurring hole through it. Such stones, which usually consist of flint, have been discovered by archaeologists in both Britain and Egypt. Commonly, they are found in Northern Germany at the coasts of the North and Baltic Seas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/H%C3%BChnergott%2C_150615%2C_ako.jpg/330px-H%C3%BChnergott%2C_150615%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adder_stone","wikidata":"Q337344"},{"title":"Choir","blocks":[{"text":"A choir, also known as a chorale or chorus, is a musical ensemble of singers. Choral music, in turn, is the music written specifically for such an ensemble to perform or in other words is the music performed by the ensemble. Choirs may perform music from the classical music repertoire, which spans from the medieval era to the present, or popular music repertoire. Most choirs are led by a conductor, who leads the performances with arm, hand, and facial gestures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg/330px-Evensong_in_York_Minster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choir","wikidata":"Q131186"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg/500px-Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg/1280px-Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg/500px-Flint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flint adder stone found on the shore of the River Thames, hung on a silk cord","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlint_hagstone_found_on_the_shore_of_the_Thames.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Egyptian_Alexandria_Jews._Choir_of_Rabbin_Moshe_Cohen_in_Samuel_Menashe_synagogue._Alexandria.jpg/500px-Egyptian_Alexandria_Jews._Choir_of_Rabbin_Moshe_Cohen_in_Samuel_Menashe_synagogue._Alexandria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Egyptian_Alexandria_Jews._Choir_of_Rabbin_Moshe_Cohen_in_Samuel_Menashe_synagogue._Alexandria.jpg/500px-Egyptian_Alexandria_Jews._Choir_of_Rabbin_Moshe_Cohen_in_Samuel_Menashe_synagogue._Alexandria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Egyptian Alexandria Jewish choir of Rabbin Moshe Cohen at Samuel Menashe synagogue, Alexandria, Egypt","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgyptian_Alexandria_Jews._Choir_of_Rabbin_Moshe_Co